# d1-08 — prompting: all options 70-100 chars, correct cycled across ranks
clear-instructions|3|A team asks Claude for "a quick look" at a supplier contract and gets general commentary rather than the exposure they were worried about.|Ask the specific question you are worried about, since a general look returns general commentary|Ask for a considerably more detailed review covering the whole of the contract|Provide the supplier's previous contract so the two can be compared directly|Ask for the review to be structured under clear headings for readability|A vague request returns a broad shallow sweep. Naming the exposure you care about directs the review to the clause that matters.|Depth and structure both improve a commentary still aimed at the wrong thing, and a comparison document widens the task rather than focusing it.
context-and-role|3|An associate asks Claude for a performance summary and it reads as though written for the employee rather than the panel who will read it.|Name the reader and the decision the summary supports, since register follows from both|Ask for a considerably more formal register in the next version produced|Provide the panel's terms of reference as background for the model to use|Ask for two versions and choose whichever of them suits the panel better|Audience and purpose determine both register and content. Naming them lets the model pitch once, correctly, rather than defaulting to the subject's frame.|A register instruction narrows without grounding, terms of reference are background rather than direction, and sampling guesses at what a sentence would state.
output-format|3|A team asks for a summary in three fixed sections and roughly one response in six merges two of them together.|State that each section appears even when it would be empty in that case|Number the three sections so that their separateness is signalled more strongly|Ask for considerably longer summaries so that merging becomes less likely|Generate each of the three sections in its own separate request instead|Sections merge when the model judges one to be thin. Stating that an empty section still gets its heading removes that judgement.|Numbering and length both nudge without deciding, and three separate calls guarantee the shape at three times the cost of one clause.
examples-fewshot|3|A team's examples all cover straightforward cases, so unusual inputs get forced into the same shape rather than handled properly.|Include an example covering an unusual case|Add an instruction about handling unusual inputs with particular care|Increase the number of straightforward examples that are supplied|Ask the model to flag any inputs that it considers to be unusual|Examples teach the boundaries of the pattern as well as the pattern itself. Demonstrating the awkward case stops it being forced into the common shape.|An instruction competes with a demonstrated pattern, more of the same examples narrows it further, and flagging identifies the problem without resolving it.
task-decomposition|4|A team asks Claude to reconcile two datasets and explain the discrepancies at once, and the explanations do not match the differences found.|Reconcile first, review the differences, then ask for explanations of those|Ask for the explanations to be considerably more rigorous within one request|Ask for the explanation to be produced before the reconciliation is done|Request that the working be shown throughout the whole of the response|Explanations generated alongside a reconciliation are written to fit the narrative rather than the data. Separating them anchors each to a verified difference.|Rigour instructions do not create the anchor, explaining before reconciling has nothing to explain, and shown working still sits inside one generation.
constraints-scope|2|A team asks for social posts within a character limit and the posts fit until hashtags and links are added afterwards.|Say the limit includes hashtags and links|Ask for shorter posts to leave some headroom for the later additions|Add the hashtags and the links by hand once the posts are within limit|Ask Claude to report the character count for each of the posts produced|Limits need their boundaries defined. Saying what counts toward the total makes the constraint match the platform's own measurement.|Headroom is guesswork, manual addition repeats forever, and a reported count measures the same wrongly-scoped total.
document-grounding|3|An associate uploads a policy and asks a question it does not address, receiving a confident answer drawn from general practice instead.|Instruct the model to answer only from the document and to say when it is silent|Ask a narrower question so the gap in the policy is less likely to be hit|Add more policy documents covering the adjacent areas of the subject|Ask for a citation to be attached to the answer that gets produced|Grounding is an instruction, not an automatic consequence of attaching a file. Without it the model fills gaps from general knowledge, invisibly.|Narrowing avoids rather than detects the gap, more documents move the boundary without exposing it, and a citation may be attached to an unsupported claim.
iteration-refinement|3|A team's refinement improved results on their own examples and nobody has checked whether it helped on anything else.|Hold back cases never used in refinement and measure against those|Add considerably more examples to the refinement set being used|Refine for rather fewer rounds in any future work on this prompt|Ask a colleague to review the prompt and give their opinion on it|Tuning against the cases you measure on inflates the measurement. A held-back set is the only way to see whether gains generalise.|A larger tuning set is still contaminated, fewer rounds leaves the same blind spot, and a colleague's read is opinion rather than measurement.
clear-instructions|3|A team asks Claude to "make the deck more persuasive" and it adds superlatives while leaving the argument structure untouched.|Say which part is unpersuasive: the claims, the evidence, or their order|Ask for a considerably more substantial rewrite of the whole deck|Provide a deck the team considers persuasive as a model to follow|Ask Claude to explain what makes a deck persuasive before it starts|Persuasion spans claims, evidence and sequencing, and the model defaults to language when the layer is unnamed. Naming it directs the work.|A bigger rewrite operates at the same layer, an exemplar teaches style rather than diagnosis, and a general explanation never touches this deck.
context-and-role|3|An associate asks for an explanation aimed at "non-technical stakeholders" and it still assumes familiarity with the company's own systems.|State which systems the readers know and which they do not, since non-technical is relative|Ask for a simpler explanation in a follow-up round after this one|Ask for every system name to be defined the first time it appears|Request an analogy-based explanation instead of a technical one|"Non-technical" is relative to a field and says nothing about internal system familiarity. Stating the actual boundary lets the model pitch it correctly.|Iterating to simplicity is slow, inline definitions patch symptoms, and analogy avoids the vocabulary without establishing what the reader knows.
output-format|3|A team needs machine-readable output and it is correct except that field values arrive with inconsistent capitalisation each run.|Specify the exact casing and validate values before the lookup runs|Ask Claude to be consistent with capitalisation across the whole output|Convert everything to lower case after the output has been received|Provide the list of permitted values so they can be matched exactly|Machine-consumed values need an exact specification plus a check, because casing is presentation to the model and load-bearing to the consumer.|A consistency instruction has no defined target, blanket lower-casing may break valid values, and a permitted list helps matching without guaranteeing form.
examples-fewshot|3|A team's examples show the desired reasoning and the model reproduces it while reaching conclusions that do not follow from it.|Include an example where the reasoning leads to an unexpected conclusion|Ask for the reasoning to be shown considerably more explicitly than now|Add more examples demonstrating the same reasoning pattern as before|Ask Claude to check its conclusion against its own stated reasoning|If every example's reasoning leads to a predictable conclusion, the model learns the conclusion rather than the reasoning. An unexpected case separates them.|Showing reasoning more explicitly reinforces the coupling, more examples deepen it, and self-checking uses the reasoning that produced the answer.
task-decomposition|3|A team asks Claude to identify problems and rank them by severity in one pass, and the ranking mirrors the order they were generated in.|Generate the problems first, then rank the reviewed list separately|Ask for the ranking step to be carried out considerably more carefully|Ask for numeric severity scores rather than a ranked ordering of them|Ask for the problems to be listed in reverse order of their severity|A ranking produced alongside generation inherits generation order rather than assessing severity. Separating them makes ranking an actual judgement.|Care instructions do not change the coupling, scores inherit the same ordering effect, and reverse order presumes the ranking already works.
constraints-scope|3|A team tells Claude the tone must stay positive and a material change affecting staff is quietly left out of the announcement.|Fix the required content first and ask for the framing separately|Ask for an announcement that is both positive and complete at once|Ask Claude to list anything that it omitted from the announcement|Switch to a neutral tone so nothing is dropped for tonal reasons|A tone constraint over unbounded scope becomes an editorial filter. Fixing required content first keeps content and framing independent.|Stacking adjectives leaves the conflict unresolved, a post-hoc omissions list is unreliable, and going neutral abandons a legitimate goal.
document-grounding|3|A researcher uploads a paper and asks about its limitations, and the answer lists generic study limitations rather than the stated ones.|Ask for the limitations the authors stated, and for a blank if they stated none|Ask for a longer answer covering considerably more limitations|Ask for the paper's limitations section to be quoted in full|Ask a more specific question about the study's methodology|Generic limitations are what a model produces when it treats the question as a topic rather than a lookup. Requiring the authors' own statement makes it retrieval.|Length produces more generic content, quoting avoids the problem rather than framing it, and method specificity narrows without redirecting to the source.
iteration-refinement|3|An associate has edited across nine messages and version nine has drifted well away from the version three the team approved.|Return to the approved version and apply the outstanding edits to that|Ask Claude to undo all the changes made since version three was approved|Ask for a summary of everything that has changed across the nine rounds|Continue with the current version and fix the drift at the very end|Iteration is lossy and drift accumulates silently. Returning to the last approved state and reapplying edits recovers the intended document.|The model cannot reliably reconstruct an earlier state, a change summary describes drift without reversing it, and continuing compounds it.
clear-instructions|3|A team asks Claude to "validate" a calculation and it confirms the arithmetic without questioning whether the formula was right.|Say which layer to validate: the arithmetic, the formula, or the inputs|Ask for a considerably more thorough validation of the calculation|Supply the underlying data that the calculation was performed on|Ask for the validation to be shown step by step in the output|"Validate" spans arithmetic, method and inputs, and arithmetic is the default reading. Naming the layer is what gets the formula questioned.|Thoroughness and step-by-step working both deepen the arithmetic check, and more data strengthens it further rather than redirecting it.
context-and-role|4|A team's prompt produces good drafts for one client and subtly wrong ones for another, and the only difference is the sector.|Pass the sector and its conventions in as an explicit input|Add considerably more detail about the first client's own sector|Ask the second client's team to adjust the drafts once produced|Ask Claude to write more generally so that both clients fit it|Prompts relying on implicit context break when the context changes. Naming the variable and passing it in makes a prompt portable across clients.|More detail about the wrong sector entrenches the mismatch, local adjustment is manual rework, and generalising strips what made the drafts useful.
output-format|2|A team asks for a numbered list of steps and several entries reference decisions made in the surrounding prose.|State that each step must stand alone without reference to other text|Ask for considerably more steps so that each one of them is smaller|Remove the surrounding prose so only the numbered steps remain|Ask Claude to check that each step is independently actionable|Independence is a property you have to require. A numbered list looks actionable while still leaning on context, so the constraint has to name it.|Granularity does not create self-containment, deleting prose leaves steps that referenced it, and self-review repeats the judgement that failed.
examples-fewshot|3|An associate's prompt includes examples written before a product rename, and outputs still use the old product name throughout.|Update the examples when the underlying facts change|Add a note to the prompt saying the product has since been renamed|Ask Claude to use the current product name rather than the old one|Reduce the number of examples that are supplied with the prompt|Examples are supplied knowledge and go stale like any other. Reviewing them on change is maintenance the technique requires.|A correction note competes with a demonstrated pattern, an instruction fights the examples directly, and fewer stale examples are still stale.
task-decomposition|3|A team asks Claude to summarise a policy and assess compliance against it at once, and the assessment covers only what the summary included.|Assess compliance against the full policy rather than against a summary|Ask for a considerably longer summary so that more of it is covered|Ask for the compliance assessment to be produced before the summary|Ask for the summary itself to be considerably more comprehensive|An assessment built on a summary inherits the summary's omissions. Working from the full source is what makes the compliance check complete.|Length reduces the omissions without removing the dependency, reordering leaves the same coupling, and comprehensiveness is the property already failing.
constraints-scope|3|A team asks Claude to keep a document under two pages and it complies by removing the appendix containing the evidence.|Say what must be preserved as well as stating the page limit|Ask for a document that is allowed to run slightly longer than two pages|Ask Claude to list whatever it removed to meet the page limit|Move the appendix into a separate file of its own instead|A size limit is a container, not its contents. Naming what must survive prevents the model choosing which section to sacrifice.|Loosening the limit invites the same choice at a larger size, a removals list arrives after the loss, and splitting is a workaround.
document-grounding|3|An associate uploads a contract and asks about termination, and the answer describes standard market practice rather than this contract.|Instruct the model to answer only from the uploaded contract|Ask the question again using considerably more specific wording|Upload the contract a second time in case it did not parse well|Ask for the termination clause to be quoted in full first|Grounding is an instruction rather than an automatic consequence of attaching a file. Without it the model blends the document with what it knows.|Rewording helps retrieval but not the blending, re-uploading addresses a failure that did not occur, and a full quote lacks the crucial instruction.
iteration-refinement|3|An associate changes a prompt and the output improves on the case they were looking at.|Check the change against all the other cases too|Keep the change, since the output clearly improved|Keep the change and watch for problems later on|Revert the change, since one case proves nothing|A fix aimed at one case can degrade others, and rerunning the rest is what shows whether it was an improvement overall.|One case is a sample of one, watching later means shipping the regression, and reverting discards a possible genuine gain.
clear-instructions|3|A team asks Claude for "options" and receives three variations on a single approach rather than genuinely different approaches.|Say the options must differ in approach, not only in their detail|Ask for considerably more options to be generated in the response|Ask Claude to make the three options rather more distinct from each other|Provide one example of a genuinely different approach to follow|Without a stated axis of variation, "options" is satisfied by variations on one theme. Naming approach as the axis produces genuine alternatives.|More options resamples the same theme, a distinctness instruction is vague about the dimension, and one example anchors rather than broadens.
context-and-role|3|An associate gives Claude a role and the answers become more confident than the evidence allows.|Drop the role, since it is adding tone rather than knowledge|Keep the role and add an instruction to be cautious|Keep the role but give it a rather more junior title|Keep the role, since it improves the answer's framing|A role shapes register, and a senior-sounding one can produce assurance the underlying answer does not actually support.|A caution instruction fights the role rather than removing it, a junior title is the same mechanism dialled down, and better framing is not better grounding.
output-format|3|A team asks Claude for a structured summary and it is correct except that dates use three different formats across the document.|State the date format explicitly and check inside the tables|Ask for the dates to be kept consistent throughout the whole document|Convert every one of the dates to plain text before they are used|Generate the tables separately with their own formatting rule|Local conventions inside structured elements are a known drift point, so an explicit format plus a targeted check where drift concentrates works.|A general consistency instruction has already implicitly failed, text conversion fights the model, and separate generation adds steps for a formatting detail.
examples-fewshot|4|A team's prompt has eight examples, and removing four of them makes no measurable difference to the accuracy of the results.|Keep the four, since the others cost tokens without earning their place|Restore all eight of them, since more examples are generally safer|Remove four more of them and keep testing further downward|Keep all eight but move them into the project configuration|If removing examples does not change accuracy, they were not contributing. Keeping the smaller set is the measured conclusion rather than a guess.|Restoring them ignores the measurement, removing further without testing risks the plateau, and relocating them reduces cost without answering whether they help.
task-decomposition|3|A team asks Claude to review a document for five separate concerns at once, and the most serious concern gets a single sentence.|Run the serious concern as its own review|Ask for a considerably longer combined review of all five concerns|List the five concerns in priority order within the prompt itself|Ask Claude to weight the most serious concern more heavily than others|Combined reviews split attention roughly evenly regardless of stakes. A dedicated pass gives a serious concern the depth it warrants.|Length raises the floor for all five, and ordering or weighting instructions nudge allocation inside a single pass without changing that it is shared.
constraints-scope|3|A team asks for a plan with no external dependencies and it complies while taking twice as long, and the constraint was a preference.|Distinguish hard requirements from preferences in the prompt|State the dependency constraint rather more softly than at present|Ask for two plans, one with the constraint and one without it|Remove the constraint and rely on review to catch problems|Everything stated as a constraint is treated as binding. Labelling the softer ones lets a plan spend a preference to buy something more valuable.|Softer phrasing is still undifferentiated, two plans is a workable fallback at double the cost, and removing it loses the signal entirely.
document-grounding|3|An associate uploads a report and asks for its recommendations, and the summary omits the conditions attached to each of them.|Ask for the conditions attached to each recommendation to be preserved|Ask for a considerably longer summary of the recommendations given|Ask for the supporting evidence for each recommendation as well|Read the recommendations section directly rather than summarising|Conditions are the qualifying material that compression removes first, and they often determine whether a recommendation applies at all.|Length helps without targeting the category, supporting evidence is a different attachment, and reading it yourself forfeits the summary.
iteration-refinement|3|A team likes a draft, asks for one small change, and gets a version where two unrelated paragraphs have also been rewritten.|Mark the rest as fixed and name only the span that may change|Ask for a considerably more conservative revision of the document|Ask Claude to list everything that it changed in the document|Ask for the change to be supplied as a separate snippet|Scope has to be marked or everything is in scope. Declaring the fixed text protects it while keeping the whole document available as context.|Conservatism is a degree rather than a boundary, a change list arrives after the edit, and a detached snippet loses the surrounding context.
clear-instructions|3|A team asks Claude to identify "any issues" with a supplier proposal and gets commercial points while the delivery risk goes unmentioned.|Ask about the specific concern and what would most undermine delivery|Ask for a considerably longer list of issues with the proposal|Ask for the issues to be ranked in order of their severity|Provide the supplier's past delivery record as background|An open request returns a shallow sweep weighted to the most commonly discussed dimension. Naming the concern is what redirects it.|Length and ranking both operate on a list generated against the wrong emphasis, and a delivery record deepens one input without redirecting the question.
context-and-role|3|A team asks Claude to draft a response to a complaint, supplying only the complaint, and the draft concedes a point the company disputes.|Supply the company's position on the disputed points first|Ask for a considerably more neutral response to be drafted|Ask Claude to identify the disputed points before it drafts|Review each of the drafts and remove concessions found|The complaint is one side's account. Without the company's position the model treats every claim as established, and a responsive reply concedes them.|Neutrality avoids the positions you want to state, identifying disputes without your position is guesswork, and review is a net rather than an input.
task-decomposition|3|A team asks Claude to write a report and check its own figures in the same request, and the check confirms the figures it produced.|Separate drafting from checking so verification is independent|Ask for the checking step to be carried out considerably more rigorously|Ask Claude to show all of its calculations within the response|Ask for the figures to be checked before the report is drafted|A check inside the same pass validates the model's own reasoning against itself, and independence is the property that makes verification meaningful.|Rigour instructions do not create independence, shown working helps a human reviewer, and reordering still leaves both steps inside one generation.
